herdr is not merely a runtime dependency here. The `herdr-protocol` crate
compiles herdr's own sources directly: most of `herdr-protocol/src` is
symlinks into the pinned `vendor/herdr` submodule, so herdr's code is linked
into the shipped HerdX binary. Those files are used unmodified.

Three files in `herdr-protocol/src` are not symlinks. They are stand-ins
written for this project, and each reproduces a small type definition from the
corresponding herdr module so that the vendored code compiles against them:

  detect.rs     the `Agent` enum and its labels
  platform.rs   one `cfg!()`-derived constant
  sound.rs      the `Sound` discriminant

HerdX.app also ships two of herdr's assets unmodified, copied from the
submodule by `scripts/bundle.sh`:

  Contents/Resources/sounds/done.mp3
  Contents/Resources/sounds/request.mp3

They are herdr's notification sounds, played here on the same agent state
changes that herdr's own client plays them on.

The submodule is pinned; `herdr-protocol/src/lib.rs` records which herdr
release, and `herdr-protocol/tests/protocol.rs` fails if upstream's
definitions drift away from the stand-ins.
